# Market Studio Cursor Rules
Project Context
Market Studio is a multi-tenant AI-powered digital marketing and lead-management platform initially focused on daycare businesses in Canada.
The platform helps daycare businesses:
•	Attract local parents
•	Capture enquiries
•	Respond quickly
•	Qualify leads
•	Book tours
•	Follow up
•	Track enrolments
•	Create and approve marketing content
•	Manage advertising campaigns
•	Measure marketing outcomes
Read and follow:
•	docs/market-studio-product-brief.md
•	docs/daycare-marketing-workflows.md
•	docs/database-design.md
These documents are the primary source of truth.
When code and documentation conflict, identify the conflict before making a major architectural change.
________________________________________
Terminology
Use the term client for a daycare business.
Do not use business, company, organization, or tenant as database table substitutes for client unless required by an external integration.
Use:
•	clients
•	client_locations
•	client_memberships
•	brand_profiles
A client may have multiple locations.
A user may belong to multiple clients.
________________________________________
Technology Stack
Use:
•	Next.js App Router
•	React
•	TypeScript
•	Supabase
•	PostgreSQL
•	Supabase Authentication
•	Supabase Storage
•	Tailwind CSS
•	Server-side authorization
•	Row Level Security
Do not introduce a new major framework without explaining why it is necessary.
Prefer built-in platform capabilities over unnecessary dependencies.
________________________________________
TypeScript Rules
•	Use TypeScript for all application code.
•	Enable strict type checking.
•	Do not use any unless unavoidable and documented.
•	Prefer unknown over any for untrusted data.
•	Validate external inputs at runtime.
•	Define shared domain types in a dedicated types module.
•	Generate or maintain Supabase database types.
•	Use enums or union types for controlled status values.
•	Avoid unsafe type assertions.
________________________________________
Code Organization
Use feature-oriented organization where practical.
Recommended structure:
src/
├── app/
├── components/
│   ├── ui/
│   └── shared/
├── features/
│   ├── auth/
│   ├── clients/
│   ├── locations/
│   ├── onboarding/
│   ├── brand-profiles/
│   ├── content/
│   ├── leads/
│   ├── conversations/
│   ├── appointments/
│   ├── campaigns/
│   ├── reports/
│   ├── integrations/
│   └── automations/
├── lib/
│   ├── supabase/
│   ├── auth/
│   ├── validation/
│   ├── permissions/
│   ├── ai/
│   └── integrations/
├── hooks/
├── types/
└── config/
Do not create excessively large components or service files.
Split files when they contain unrelated responsibilities.
________________________________________
Server and Client Components
•	Prefer Server Components by default.
•	Use Client Components only when browser interactivity is required.
•	Keep database access on the server.
•	Never expose service-role credentials to Client Components.
•	Use server actions or route handlers for secure mutations.
•	Validate authorization during every server-side mutation.
•	Do not rely only on hidden buttons or protected routes.
________________________________________
Supabase Rules
•	Use separate browser and server Supabase clients.
•	Use the service-role client only in trusted server environments.
•	Never expose the service-role key to the browser.
•	Enable Row Level Security on all client-owned tables.
•	Every client-owned record must contain client_id.
•	Every location-owned record should contain location_id.
•	Add foreign keys and indexes.
•	Use migrations for all schema changes.
•	Do not make undocumented manual production schema changes.
•	Update generated TypeScript types after schema changes.
________________________________________
Multi-Tenant Security
Every feature must be designed for multi-tenancy.
For every query or mutation, verify:
1.	The authenticated user exists.
2.	The user has an active client membership.
3.	The user has the required client role.
4.	Location-level access is valid when applicable.
5.	The target record belongs to the same client.
Never accept a browser-provided client_id as proof of authorization.
Resolve access using the authenticated user’s memberships.
Prevent all cross-client data access.
________________________________________
Role and Permission Rules
Support these platform and client roles:
Platform roles
•	super_admin
•	market_studio_admin
•	account_manager
•	client_user
Client roles
•	client_admin
•	location_manager
•	staff
•	viewer
•	market_studio_account_manager
Use centralized permission helpers.
Do not scatter hard-coded role checks throughout UI components.
Permissions should be based on actions such as:
•	client.read
•	client.update
•	lead.read
•	lead.update
•	content.approve
•	campaign.manage
•	appointment.manage
•	report.read
________________________________________
Database Naming
Use:
•	Lowercase snake_case
•	Plural table names
•	Singular descriptive field names
•	UUID primary keys
•	timestamptz timestamps
•	created_at
•	updated_at
Foreign-key fields should end in _id.
Examples:
•	client_id
•	location_id
•	lead_id
•	campaign_id
________________________________________
Validation
Validate all:
•	Forms
•	Route parameters
•	Search parameters
•	API payloads
•	Webhooks
•	AI outputs
•	Integration responses
Use a runtime validation library such as Zod.
Return field-specific validation errors where practical.
Do not trust third-party webhook payloads.
________________________________________
Error Handling
•	Use structured errors.
•	Provide user-friendly messages in the UI.
•	Log technical details on the server.
•	Never expose credentials, tokens, stack traces, or internal database details.
•	Record automation failures in automation_runs.
•	Prevent duplicate execution during retries.
•	Use idempotency keys for external actions when supported.
________________________________________
AI Rules
AI is an assistant, not the source of truth.
AI-generated content must be based on approved client information.
AI must not invent:
•	Daycare availability
•	Fees
•	Licensing status
•	Subsidy eligibility
•	Staff qualifications
•	Safety claims
•	Program details
•	Opening dates
•	Contact information
AI outputs must be validated before storage or execution.
Store:
•	Agent type
•	Task type
•	Model identifier
•	Prompt version
•	Relevant input
•	Structured output
•	Approval status
•	Execution status
Do not store hidden chain-of-thought reasoning.
Store concise summaries, classifications, recommendations, and outputs only.
________________________________________
Human Approval Rules
Require human approval for:
•	Public content publication
•	Negative review responses
•	Sensitive parent communication
•	Significant advertising-budget changes
•	Licensing claims
•	Subsidy claims
•	Guaranteed availability
•	Incident-related communication
•	Enrolment decisions
•	Use of identifiable child images
Routine acknowledgement messages and reminders may be automated when approved templates and consent rules are configured.
________________________________________
Privacy Rules
The platform serves Canadian businesses.
Design for privacy and consent requirements.
•	Store communication consent history.
•	Store consent source and timestamp.
•	Support withdrawal and unsubscribe requests.
•	Avoid unnecessary personal-data collection.
•	Avoid storing child medical or developmental information.
•	Restrict access to parent contact information.
•	Use private storage for sensitive files.
•	Support data export and deletion workflows.
•	Do not include sensitive personal information in logs.
•	Do not send marketing messages without an appropriate consent basis.
________________________________________
UI and UX Rules
The interface should be:
•	Professional
•	Clean
•	Simple
•	Responsive
•	Accessible
•	Easy for non-technical daycare staff
Use consistent page patterns.
Every major list should support relevant:
•	Search
•	Filters
•	Sorting
•	Pagination
•	Empty states
•	Loading states
•	Error states
Avoid overcrowded dashboards.
Prioritize actionable information.
For example, show:
•	Leads requiring follow-up
•	Tours happening today
•	Content awaiting approval
•	Failed automations
•	Campaign risks
•	New recommendations
________________________________________
Forms
•	Use clear labels.
•	Mark required fields.
•	Show validation near the relevant field.
•	Preserve entered values after validation errors.
•	Confirm destructive actions.
•	Use sensible defaults.
•	Use searchable selects for large datasets.
•	Do not request information that is unnecessary for the workflow.
________________________________________
Accessibility
•	Use semantic HTML.
•	Support keyboard navigation.
•	Provide visible focus states.
•	Add accessible labels.
•	Use sufficient contrast.
•	Add alt text for meaningful images.
•	Do not communicate status using colour alone.
•	Ensure forms are usable with assistive technologies.
________________________________________
API and Integration Rules
•	Keep provider-specific logic inside integration modules.
•	Convert provider payloads into Market Studio domain models.
•	Do not spread Meta, Google, email, or SMS field names throughout the application.
•	Verify webhook signatures.
•	Handle rate limits.
•	Implement retry policies.
•	Store external identifiers.
•	Record sync errors.
•	Prevent duplicate webhook processing.
•	Keep secrets in environment variables or secure secret storage.
________________________________________
Testing Rules
Write tests for high-risk logic, including:
•	Tenant isolation
•	Role permissions
•	Lead duplicate detection
•	Lead routing
•	Consent enforcement
•	Content approval
•	Appointment availability
•	Automation idempotency
•	Webhook validation
•	Conversion attribution
Before completing a feature, verify:
•	Happy path
•	Unauthorized access
•	Invalid input
•	Empty state
•	Integration failure
•	Duplicate submission
•	Retry behavior
________________________________________
Migration Rules
For every database change:
1.	Create a migration.
2.	Add foreign keys.
3.	add indexes where needed.
4.	enable or update Row Level Security.
5.	update TypeScript database types.
6.	update docs/database-design.md.
7.	explain any data migration requirement.
Never silently rename or delete production columns.
________________________________________
Implementation Approach
When asked to build a new feature:
1.	Read the relevant project documents.
2.	State the assumptions being used.
3.	identify affected database tables.
4.	identify required permissions.
5.	identify pages and components.
6.	identify server actions or API routes.
7.	identify automation or integration dependencies.
8.	implement in small, testable steps.
9.	include loading, empty, and error states.
10.	update documentation where needed.
Do not implement a large feature as one unstructured file.
________________________________________
MVP Priority
Prioritize:
1.	Authentication
2.	Client management
3.	Location management
4.	Client memberships
5.	Brand profiles
6.	Onboarding
7.	Lead capture
8.	Lead pipeline
9.	Lead activities
10.	Automated acknowledgements
11.	Follow-up tasks
12.	Tour scheduling
13.	Content calendar
14.	Content approval
15.	Reporting
16.	Audit and automation logs
Avoid building non-MVP features unless explicitly requested.
________________________________________
Definition of Done
A feature is complete only when:
•	Types are defined.
•	Inputs are validated.
•	Authorization is enforced.
•	Tenant isolation is enforced.
•	Loading states exist.
•	Empty states exist.
•	Error states exist.
•	Relevant actions are audited.
•	Tests cover high-risk logic.
•	Documentation is updated.
•	No secrets are exposed.
•	The feature works for multiple clients and locations.
